    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "whenever (1.3) is satisfied. This gives a more satisfactory concept of a quantum limiting distribution than in [7] where quantum ergodicity was assessed by the behaviour of eigenvector bases, and it was shown in [7, §4.5] that such a limiting distribution depends on the eigenvector basis. In contrast, here the RHS of (1.6) depends only on the graph."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 30,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 85.039, 148.252, 510.239, 212.821 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Our main target now is to compute the weights d(p,q) for specific finite graphs GF. Because of point (4) above, the theorems are illustrated only for the Cartesian product, but some hold more generally. For definiteness, the reader can assume Γ0 = Z in all these results, which is already interesting. However, nothing changes for any Γ0 having a single vertex in its fundamental domain, such as Zd and the triangular lattice."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 31,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 85.039, 98.301, 510.242, 148.064 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "A nice simplification in the family of Cartesian products is that the limiting weight d(p,q) in (1.6) depends only on the finite graph GF, compared to the general case (1.5), where the weight depends on the full Floquet matrix and computations become more daunting. Still, as we will see, Cartesian products already offer interesting contrasting"
  } ]
